{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, pragmatic decision rule based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness), where 'preference' denotes a methodological commitment to the theory with the highest content that has not yet been falsified, rather than a probabilistic expectation of future success.","counterconditions":["If Popper's own concession that verisimilitude cannot be formally defined renders the metric unusable for rational choice.","If the act of preferring a theory for action implicitly assumes a causal link between past survival and future reliability.","If the distinction between 'decision' and 'inductive belief' collapses under the pressure of practical necessity."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reinterprets 'preference' not as an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past, but as a logical comparison of logical content and test severity. The 'better' theory is simply the one that has survived more severe tests (higher informational content), making it the rational choice for further testing and application without assigning it higher probability. This relies on the distinction between the logical status of the theory (unchanged) and the meta-scientific decision to adopt the most informative survivor.","optional_refs":["5f8544e48088da3dbf5a97f5348c06bd4662fbe5cd67a2a851fbe6dbd935b3c2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system, rendering his rejection of probabilistic confirmation inconsistent with scientific practice, because any rational preference for action logically entails an expectation of future success which can only be grounded in the inductive assumption that past performance predicts future results.","counterconditions":["If 'preference' can be decoupled from 'expectation of success' and redefined purely as a procedural rule.","If the cost of abandoning induction is shown to be the abandonment of rational action itself.","If Popper's system allows for 'blind' preference where the agent admits they have no reason to expect success but chooses anyway."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Analyzes the logical structure of 'rational preference.' Argues that to prefer X over Y for the purpose of achieving a goal Z implies a belief that X is more likely to achieve Z than Y. Since Popper denies that corroboration increases probability, the only remaining basis for this belief is an implicit inductive principle: that the properties which allowed X to survive past tests will persist. Thus, the 'pragmatic' defense is a disguised form of induction.","optional_refs":["5debf7b4d53013ec19c467ebcc224f05caf30aaba01604db9920b0a1dad2e07f"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resolution lies in reinterpreting corroboration as an evolutionary selection mechanism where 'preference' is a survival trait of the methodology, not a logical inference about the theory's truth, thereby dissolving the need for induction by shifting the locus of justification from the individual theory to the meta-methodology.","counterconditions":["If evolutionary success is not correlated with truth-likeness (pessimistic meta-induction).","If the mechanism requires assuming that the environment (nature) is stable enough for selection to work.","If this view reduces scientific rationality to mere biological adaptation."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Draws on evolutionary epistemology. The preference for well-tested theories is not a logical deduction about the world's structure but a methodological rule that has itself been 'corroborated' by the survival of science. We prefer them because the *method* of preferring them has allowed our scientific frameworks to survive. This shifts the justification from the theory (conjectural) to the meta-methodology (survival of the fittest ideas).","optional_refs":["1cc757de7d755c213decb665865118f54ad52a4636c4ec03514f37f51f6314fe"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves if we treat the preference for corroborated theories as a convention or decision postulate, similar to choosing axioms, rather than a derived logical consequence, accepting that the rationality of science is internal to the game of science.","counterconditions":["If the decision itself requires an inductive justification to be considered rational.","If scientists cannot coherently maintain a stance that their actions have no expected link to truth.","If this conventionalism undermines the objective status of scientific knowledge."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper explicitly frames the choice of the best-tested theory as a 'decision.' There is no logical bridge between 'survived tests' and 'should be used.' The bridge is built by a voluntary commitment to a rule of procedure: 'We decide to act as if the best-tested theory is the most reliable.' This avoids induction by making no claim about the world guaranteeing success; it only establishes a normative rule.","optional_refs":["03f8b3f2f0045799db01bdb5c40524ddb0adf4dcd784fe98187408e4f273c86b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if we accept a 'critical rationalist' definition of rationality that divorces it from justification entirely, viewing the preference for corroborated theories as the only non-arbitrary option remaining after all others have been critically eliminated.","counterconditions":["If the set of possible theories is infinite, making elimination incomplete.","If 'non-arbitrary' implicitly relies on an inductive assumption that the eliminated patterns won't recur.","If this negative definition of rationality is too weak to guide actual technological application."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Redefines rationality not as 'having positive reasons/beliefs' (which leads to induction) but as 'avoiding error.' We prefer the corroborated theory not because we believe it will work, but because we have critically eliminated the alternatives that failed. The preference is the residue of criticism. It is the 'last man standing' approach. This avoids induction because it makes no positive claim about the survivor, only negative claims about the losers.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The problem is a category error: Popper's 'preference' is a heuristic for generating new problems, not a guide for action. When scientists 'act' on theories, they leave the realm of pure Popperian science and enter engineering, where induction is pragmatically unavoidable and admitted.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly claimed his system guides action (which he did).","If the separation between 'testing' and 'acting' is porous in real scientific practice.","If admitting induction in engineering undermines the philosophical purity of the scientific method."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Bifurcates the domain. In 'Science Proper' (Popper's domain), preference is only for the purpose of further testing (seeking falsification). In 'Engineering/Application', agents necessarily use induction to survive. Popper's system is consistent if we admit it does not cover the transition to action. The 'smuggling' happens only when we demand science to do what it wasn't designed to do: guarantee safety/success.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25}],"simulation_proposals":[]}